     DisplayDeviceConfig for the built-in panel.

     ── What this file is for ────────────────────────────────────────────────

     Exactly one thing: the auto-brightness debounce. Everything else is
     deliberately absent, and that is load-bearing rather than lazy — see
     "Why it is this small" below.

     Without this file the device runs auto-brightness with NO debounce at all.
     Measured on the device before writing it:

         adb shell dumpsys display | grep LightDebounce
           mAutoBrightnessBrighteningLightDebounce = -1
           mAutoBrightnessDarkeningLightDebounce   = -1
           mBrighteningLightDebounceConfig         = -1
           mDarkeningLightDebounceConfig           = -1

     -1 is INVALID_AUTO_BRIGHTNESS_LIGHT_DEBOUNCE, the field initialiser at
     DisplayDeviceConfig.java:806-819. It reaches AutomaticBrightnessController
     unchanged (DisplayPowerController.java:1136-1143) and is used as an
     arithmetic offset, not checked:

         AutomaticBrightnessController.java:891-892
             return earliestValidTime + mBrighteningLightDebounceConfig;

     so the "next allowed transition" is always one millisecond in the PAST and
     every single lux sample is allowed to move the backlight. That is the
     jumpy-auto-brightness symptom, and it is a defect rather than a preference.

     ⚠️ The obvious fix does not work, and the tree tried it. There ARE two
     framework resources for this and FrameworkOverlayMalbec used to set them to
     2000/3000; `cmd overlay lookup android android:integer/
     config_autoBrightnessBrighteningLightDebounce` on the running device
     correctly returns 2000. They were still dead, because the ONLY caller of
     loadAutoBrightnessBrighteningLightDebounce() is loadAutoBrightnessConfigValues()
     (:2467-2472), which runs from initFromFile() alone. The config.xml path,
     initFromGlobalXml() (:1947-1959), calls loadAutoBrightnessConfigsFromConfigXml()
     — which builds only the brightness mapping and never touches the debounce.
     So the resource fallback inside that loader exists to serve a DDC file that
     omits the tag; it is not reachable without a DDC file at all. A device with
     no DisplayDeviceConfig XML cannot have a debounce, whatever it writes in
     config.xml. This file is the whole fix.

     ── Why port_147 and not an id ───────────────────────────────────────────

     loadConfigFromDirectory (:1052-1075) tries three names in order:
     display_id_<physicalDisplayId>, display_<id-without-stable-bit>, and
     display_port_<port>. The first two encode the PANEL MODEL; the third does
     not. This tablet ships with either a BOE nt36536e or a CSOT nt36536 panel
     and the tree is not allowed to depend on which (see the note at the top of
     BoardConfig.mk), so the port form is the only one that can be correct for
     both. Port 147 is measured, not assumed:

         adb shell dumpsys display | grep 'address {port'
           address {port=147, model=0x40446d48e02993}

     It also means the three stock display_id_*.xml blobs still win for the units
     they name, which is what should happen — those carry a full brightness map
     and this file deliberately does not.

     ── Why it is this small ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

     A DisplayDeviceConfig file REPLACES config.xml piecemeal, per section, and
     the sections this file omits keep their current, verified values:

       * loadBrightnessMap (:1873-1879) explicitly falls back to
         loadBrightnessMapFromConfigXml() when <screenBrightnessMap> is absent,
         so config_screenBrightnessNits / config_screenBrightnessBacklight in
         FrameworkOverlayMalbec still define the curve. Nothing about the
         brightness behaviour changes.
       * loadBrightnessDefaultFromDdcXml (:2010-2024) does the same for
         <screenBrightnessDefault>.
       * loadBrightnessConstraintsFromConfigXml() is called on the file path too
         (:1888), so config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimumFloat = 0.003937008
         survives — that is the value that stops the panel reaching backlight 0
         and going black, and losing it would be the expensive mistake here.

     The generated parser tolerates every one of those omissions by construction:
     DisplayConfiguration.read() is a flat tag-dispatch loop over the children
     present, and the only exception it can raise is "DisplayConfiguration is not
     closed" for malformed XML. Unset elements stay null. (Read out of the built
     parser at out/soong/.intermediates/frameworks/base/services/core/xsd/
     display-device-config/, not assumed from the .xsd — the .xsd marks
     screenBrightnessMap as required and the parser does not enforce it.)

     ── HDR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     Owner's decision: keep the default SDR brightness, but HDR must work. Those
     two are separable, and <hdrBrightnessConfig> is exactly the separation.

     Before this block, mHdrBrightnessData was null on the device. HdrBrightnessData
     .loadConfig (:173-179) falls back to the <highBrightnessMode> block when
     there is no <hdrBrightnessConfig>, and with neither present it produced
     nothing, so HDR content was displayed with no brightness headroom at all —
     tone-mapped down into the SDR range instead of being allowed above it.

     ★ The reason this does not touch SDR: with no <highBrightnessMode> block,
     getTransitionPoint (:211-215) returns PowerManager.BRIGHTNESS_MAX. So HDR
     may boost up to the top of the panel's existing range and no further, and
     the SDR curve — which still comes from config_screenBrightnessNits, because
     <screenBrightnessMap> is absent — is untouched. Adding HBM instead would
     have moved the SDR ceiling too, which is what the owner said not to do.

     Every child element below is mandatory in practice, not by taste:
     loadConfig dereferences getBrightnessMap(), getBrightnessIncreaseDebounceMillis(),
     getBrightnessDecreaseDebounceMillis(), getScreenBrightnessRampIncrease() and
     getScreenBrightnessRampDecrease() unconditionally (:181-207). Omitting any
     one of them is an NPE inside DisplayDeviceConfig at boot, not a fallback.

     brightnessMap is lux -> maximum brightness allowed for HDR, and the lookup
     (HdrBrightnessModifier.findBrightnessLimit:380-399) takes the SMALLEST key
     GREATER than the current ambient lux. Above the highest key it stays at the
     initialiser, PowerManager.BRIGHTNESS_MAX — unlimited. So this is a cap for
     dim rooms only, which is the point: HDR highlights at full panel output in
     a dark room are painful, and in daylight there is nothing to protect
     against. The two points are AOSP's own documented example values
     (HdrBrightnessData.java:39-51); they are a starting shape, not a
     measurement, and the honest way to improve them is to watch real HDR
     content at night.

     sdrHdrRatioMap has to live HERE rather than being inherited: getSdrHdrRatioSpline
     (:212-219) falls back to the HBM block's map, and there is no HBM block, so
     without this element the spline is null and the headroom is 1.0 — i.e. no
     boost, which is the state being fixed.

     ★ THE 120 POINTS BELOW ARE LENOVO'S MEASURED CURVE FOR THIS PANEL, AND THE
     TWO THEY REPLACE WERE THE WRONG PANEL'S. Corrected session 34.

     This element used to carry two points, 2 nits at 8.0x falling to 500 nits at
     1.5x, described here as "Lenovo's own, lifted from the sdrHdrRatioMap inside
     the highBrightnessMode block of their display_id_*.xml". ⚠️ And the section
     below this one proves, correctly and at length, that display_id_*.xml is
     keyed to panel models this unit does not have. The file diagnosed its own
     source as wrong and took two numbers out of it anyway.

     ★ The file that stock actually used was never examined, because it is on
     /system and every search for panel data went to /vendor:

         work/unpacked/parts/system/system/etc/display_hdr.xml   14,060 B, 120 points

     Stock's live dumpsys matches it exactly (minLux 10000.0, transition 1.0,
     1800/300/60 timings, minimumHdrPercentOfScreen 0.1, a 120-point spline).
     What the two curves actually differed by, computed rather than argued:

         SDR nits      Lenovo measured      the two points
             50            4.00x               7.37x
            100            3.26x               6.72x
            150            2.52x               6.07x
            300            1.79x               4.11x
            500            1.62x               1.50x

     At the brightness a 13 inch tablet sits at indoors we were granting roughly
     double the headroom Lenovo measured, so HDR highlights drove the panel about
     twice as hard as it was tuned for. The old curve even started at 8.0x where
     Lenovo's starts at 4.0x, which is the clearest sign the two points were not
     this panel's to begin with.

     allowInLowPowerMode is false: Battery Saver should not be spending its
     savings on HDR highlights.

     ── Deliberately NOT here, and why ───────────────────────────────────────

     The stock blobs carry a 52-point map ending at 782.4283 nits plus an
     <highBrightnessMode> block, while this device's live ladder stops at 650
     nits and has no HBM. Copying that in would raise the brightness ceiling by
     20% — and it is not this panel's curve. All three stock files are
     byte-identical (md5 e78fa6de4b6f0bef0959fefe6d105219) and are keyed to
     models 0x446dcbf35481 (ports 130, 131) and 0x446dd4433735 (port 147); this
     unit is model 0x446d48e02993. So none of them matched on stock ZUI either,
     which means the 650-nit ladder IS the stock behaviour of this unit and
     raising it would be a change, not a restoration.

     ★★ AND NEITHER CAN display_hdr.xml's <highBrightnessMode> BLOCK BE ADOPTED,
     for a reason that is structural rather than a preference. Established
     session 34, before writing anything, and it would otherwise have cost a
     build, a flash and a boot.

     Session 34 set out to adopt that block whole, on the reasoning that its
     <transitionPoint> is 1 rather than display_id_*.xml's 0.85, so it would give
     the sunlight HBM trigger (minimumLux 10000) without moving the SDR ceiling.
     The premise is right and the conclusion is wrong, because AOSP will not
     accept the value at all — DisplayDeviceConfig.java:1896-1903:

         float transitionPointBacklightScale = hbm.getTransitionPoint_all().floatValue();
         if (transitionPointBacklightScale >= mBacklightMaximum) {
             throw new IllegalArgumentException("HBM transition point invalid. "
                 + transitionPointBacklightScale + " is not less than " + mBacklightMaximum);
         }

     mBacklightMaximum is PowerManager.BRIGHTNESS_MAX, i.e. 1.0, whenever there
     is no <screenBrightnessMap> (:1972) — which is this file's case. So
     transitionPoint 1 is `1 >= 1.0`, and the block throws inside
     DisplayDeviceConfig while the display is being configured.

     ★ Stock gets away with it because ZUI's framework reads
     /system/etc/display_hdr.xml through Lenovo's own loader, not through AOSP's
     DisplayDeviceConfig. The file is valid for the parser that was written for
     it and invalid for ours.

     ★★ And the generalisation is the part worth keeping: ANY transitionPoint
     AOSP accepts is strictly less than 1.0, and
     HighBrightnessModeController.java:195 returns mHbmData.transitionPoint from
     getCurrentBrightnessMax() whenever HBM is not currently engaged. So an
     accepted HBM block necessarily LOWERS the everyday SDR ceiling to the
     transition point. HBM and "do not move the SDR ceiling" are mutually
     exclusive in AOSP, whatever the value. The owner's constraint therefore
     rules out the whole element, not merely one number in it, and the sunlight
     trigger cannot be had on this framework without giving up SDR headroom.

     What was adoptable was the curve, and it is adopted above.

     Whether this panel can actually do 782 nits is unsettled. The composer
     reports HdrCapabilities mMaxLuminance=800.0, which is suggestive and is not
     proof — it is the HDR peak, not the SDR ceiling. Settling it needs a
     luminance measurement or a panel datasheet, and inventing the number would
     be worse than the 650 that is there now.
-->
